  "lines": [
    {
      "text": "Pearls rarely start with sand.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "hook",
        "show": "clam",
        "color": "#5bb4e8"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "The old story says a grain of sand starts a pearl.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "title",
        "text": "The old story",
        "sub": "A grain of sand",
        "color": "#f7c23a"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Usually the trigger is a parasite or an injury.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "cast",
        "setting": "lab",
        "name": "A marine biologist",
        "role": "Usually a parasite or an injury",
        "cast": [
          {
            "outfit": "coat",
            "outfitColor": "#2f6f7a",
            "hair": "updo",
            "hairColor": "#3a2a1e",
            "skin": "#d9a87e",
            "expression": "smile"
          }
        ],
        "color": "#4cc3a8"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "The mollusk wraps it in thin layers of nacre.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "title",
        "text": "Nacre",
        "sub": "Layer after layer",
        "color": "#ec6a9c"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Nacre is also called mother-of-pearl.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "reveal",
        "item": "round-pearl",
        "label": "Nacre",
        "color": "#a98be0"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "It is made of a mineral called aragonite plus a protein.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "title",
        "text": "Aragonite",
        "sub": "A mineral, plus a protein",
        "color": "#5bb4e8"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Many shells are lined with the same shimmery nacre.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "reveal",
        "item": "clam",
        "label": "Lined with nacre",
        "color": "#f7c23a"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Pearls grow in saltwater oysters and freshwater mussels.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "title",
        "text": "Oysters and mussels",
        "sub": "Salt water and fresh",
        "color": "#c8dc3c"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Oysters have even entombed a whole fish in nacre.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "cast",
        "setting": "study",
        "name": "A museum curator",
        "role": "A fish entombed in nacre",
        "cast": [
          {
            "outfit": "suit",
            "outfitColor": "#5a4a6a",
            "hair": "side",
            "hairColor": "#8a8a8a",
            "skin": "#efc9a6",
            "expression": "focused",
            "facial": "beard"
          }
        ],
        "color": "#ec6a9c"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "On pearl farms, a technician usually adds a small shell bead.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "title",
        "text": "A shell bead",
        "sub": "Placed in the oyster",
        "color": "#f0552e"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Natural pearls are rare, and most sold today are cultured.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "reveal",
        "item": "round-pearl",
        "label": "Cultured",
        "color": "#4cc3a8"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "A pearl can take months to several years to form.",
      "scene": {
        "kind": "jewel",
        "piece": "necklace",
        "tone": "#f4efe6",
        "metal": "yellow",
        "label": "Months to several years",
        "color": "#5bb4e8"
      }
    }
  ],
  "post": {
    "caption": "Pearls rarely start with sand. The old story says a grain of sand starts a pearl, but usually the trigger is a parasite or an injury. The mollusk wraps it in thin layers of nacre, also called mother-of-pearl, made of a mineral called aragonite plus a protein. Pearls grow in saltwater oysters and freshwater mussels, and on pearl farms a technician usually adds a small shell bead. A pearl can take months to several years to form.",
